  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 226 PM MDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nThrough Friday evening and overnight, a second disturbance behind \nthe tropical remnants look to spark scattered to numerous showers \nand storms across portions of western and central NM. Currently, \nhighest rainfall amounts are focused in the higher terrain in Catron \nCounty, though these amounts currently sit under 1\" as this \ndisturbance is expected to be rather weak and short lived. A few \nscattered showers and storms look to remain Saturday across the \nnorthern mountains, though overall it looks to be a down day between \nsystems. \n\nAttention then shifts to Sunday and into the beginning of next week \nwith the approach of two disturbances: one from the Pacific \nNorthwest and one from off the coast of California. These two \ndisturbances look to phase together at some point this weekend as\nthey begin to move east and southeast across the Desert \nSouthwest. How exactly they phase and move will have a fairly \nsignificant impact on the forecast for our area. Recent GFS runs \nhave dragged the PNW low through WY/CO with the trough axis \nscraping the northern third of the state. The ECMWF runs have \nbrought the PNW low into northern AZ and NM. The GFS runs \ncorrelate to slightly lower precipitation amounts across portions \nof western NM as the upslope flow component shifts dramatically \ngiven the orientation of the flow. The ECMWF runs favor a better \nsetup for upslope flow and orographically enhanced precipitation \namounts. Both solutions do favor widespread rain and embedded \nthunderstorms across western and central NM Sunday into Monday. \nWhile there are still the finer details to settle out, there looks\nto be at least decent chances for flooding impacts to return to \nareas that were impacted this past week. As it stands now, \nrainfall amounts are most likely to stand in the 0.5-1.5\" range, \nwith those higher amounts likely occurring near terrain with \nupslope flow. These amounts are currently less than what \npreviously occurred across the area, however given the saturated \nnature of the soils, runoff is likely to develop quickly. \nInitially, this round of rain does not look to have very heavy \nrates, but a steady moderate rain may work to induce additional \nrounds of river flooding. On top of rain, snow levels look to \nremain around 10,000ft or so, and some of the higher peaks of the \nnorthern mountains may pick up a couple to several of inches of \nsnow, especially the Tusas Mountains.\n\nOnce clarity comes through on the progression of the trough, this \nwill dictate the weather going into the first week of November. \nCurrently, troughing looks to set up in the south central Great \nPlains, which would put stiff north to northwest flow over the \nstate. Surface winds would trend up in this scenario, and overall \ncooler temperatures and drier conditions would encompass the\nstate.",
